<h1><span class="part">Part A</span>The ideas you need</h1>

## A1. What a data center is

A **data center** is a building full of computers called **servers**. Companies send
them work: "run this program". Our simulated data center has **64 servers** of nearly
the same size. For this project, two parts of a server matter:

- **CPU**: the part that calculates. Each of our servers has **64 CPU cores** (64 small
  calculators working side by side).
- **Memory** (RAM): the space where a running program keeps its data.

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Analogy: a kitchen</span>
Think of a server as a kitchen. The **CPU cores are the cooks** and the **memory is the
counter space**. Every dish (a task) needs some cooks and some counter space. If the
counter is full, you cannot start another dish, even if cooks are standing around.
That is exactly what happens on our servers, and it matters later.
</div>

## A2. Power and energy

Two words that sound alike but mean different things:

| Word | Unit | Meaning | Everyday example |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Power** | watt (W) | how fast electricity is used *right now* | a 60 W light bulb |
| **Energy** | kilowatt-hour (kWh) | power **×** time: the total used | the number on the electricity bill |

**1 kWh = 1,000 W for 1 hour**, about a microwave running for one hour.

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Tiny example</span>
A server drawing 100 W for 3 hours uses 100 W × 3 h = 300 Wh = **0.3 kWh**.
Two servers drawing 100 W each for 3 hours use **0.6 kWh**.
</div>

The goal of the whole project is **fewer kWh for the same work**, without making the
work noticeably slower.

## A3. The three states of a server

The simulator (Brave's engine, `ecosched/engine.py`) puts every server in exactly
one of three states at every moment. These are the numbers for the HPE server model:

| State | Power | What it means |
|---|---:|---|
| **Asleep** | {{ v.sleep_w }} W | cannot run anything, almost free |
| **Waking up** | {{ v.full_w }} W for {{ v.wake_s }} s | full power while it boots; its tasks wait |
| **On** | {{ v.idle_w }} W and up | runs tasks; draws more the busier its CPU is |

Two rules complete the picture:

1. An **on server with no work falls asleep after {{ v.idle_timeout }} seconds**.
2. A task can only start on a server that is on. If the scheduler picks a sleeping
   server, it must wake up first, so the task **waits {{ v.wake_s }} seconds**.

<div class="box why" markdown="1">
<span class="label">The most important number in this guide</span>
Being on costs **{{ v.on_premium }} W more than sleeping** ({{ v.idle_w }} − {{ v.sleep_w }}),
even with nothing to do. So **every server that is on but could be asleep wastes
{{ v.on_premium }} W**. Saving energy is mostly about having fewer servers on.
</div>

## A4. The power curve

How much power does an *on* server draw? That depends on how busy its CPU is. Real
servers were measured with a standard benchmark called **SPECpower**, which records the
watts at 0%, 10%, 20%, …, 100% CPU load. That list is the server's **power curve**.
The team uses three real server models: HPE DL360 Gen9, Huawei RH2288H V3 and
QuantaGrid D51B-2U. Here is the HPE curve:

{{ t.power_table }}

Read the table in three baby steps:

1. **Watts** go up as the CPU gets busier, from {{ v.idle_w }} W (idle) to {{ v.full_w }} W (100%).
2. **Cost of the next 10%** is not the same everywhere. The **first 10% is the most
   expensive slice** ({{ v.first_slice }} W). Slices in the middle are cheap
   (as low as {{ v.cheapest_slice }} W).
3. **Watts per unit of work** divides the watts by the load. It is the price of doing
   one "full server" worth of work at that load. It falls from {{ v.eff_10 }} W at 10% load
   to about {{ v.eff_best }} W at {{ v.eff_best_load }} load. **Lightly loaded servers
   are very wasteful.**

<figure>
<img src="charts/power_curve.svg" alt="HPE DL360 Gen9 power curve and the cost of each 10% slice">
<figcaption>Figure 1. Left: HPE watts at each CPU load. Right: watts added by each 10% slice. The first slice is by far the most expensive.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Tiny example: packing versus spreading</span>
You have work equal to 20% of one server's CPU, and two servers.

| | Server A | Server B | Total |
|---|---|---|---:|
| **Pack** it on one server | 20% → {{ v.p20 }} W | asleep → {{ v.sleep_w }} W | **{{ v.pack_total }} W** |
| **Spread** it over two | 10% → {{ v.p10 }} W | 10% → {{ v.p10 }} W | **{{ v.spread_total }} W** |

Same work, but spreading costs **{{ v.spread_extra }} W more** ({{ v.spread_pct }} more).
Of that, {{ v.on_premium }} W is server B being on instead of asleep, and
{{ v.spread_dynamic }} W is paying for the expensive first slice twice.
</div>

## A5. Tasks and the Alibaba trace

In 2017 Alibaba published a **trace**: a log of 12 hours of real work in one of its data
centers. The team turned it into simulator input (`RAW_Alibaba/`).

- A **job** is split into **tasks**. A task asks for some **CPU** (in cores) and some
  **memory**, and runs for a recorded number of seconds. What it asks for is its
  **request**.
- Tasks that belong together form a **task group**. The team made three test sizes:
  **250, 1,000 and 4,000 groups** (small, medium, large). The large one has
  {{ v.team_tasks_4000 }} tasks in 12 hours.
- A **typical task** asks for half a core ({{ v.task_cpu_pct }} of a server's CPU) and
  {{ v.task_mem_pct }} of its memory. In kitchen terms, it needs few cooks but a fair amount of counter
  space, so **memory tends to run out before CPU does**.
- The team labels each group by its shape: **balanced**, **memory-heavy** or
  **CPU-heavy**.

The simulator replays the tasks exactly: same arrival times, same requests, same recorded
durations. Only the scheduling rule changes between runs, so any difference in energy
comes from the rule alone.

## A6. Scheduling: two decisions

A **scheduler** makes two kinds of decisions:

1. **Placement.** A new task arrives. Which server should run it?
2. **Migration.** Later, move a running task to another server. While its memory is
   copied over the network, the task pauses for a few seconds.

Tasks are placed in arrival order (**first in, first out**). If the next task fits on no
server, every task behind it waits too. A task's **wait** is the time from arrival to
start. Usually it is 0; it is {{ v.wake_s }} s if a server had to wake up; it is longer only
when the whole data center is full.

<figure>
<img src="charts/life_of_a_task.svg" alt="How a task and a server move through the simulator">
<figcaption>Figure 2. The life of a task (top) and of a server (bottom). The controller runs every 5 minutes.</figcaption>
</figure>

## A7. Packing and consolidation

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Analogy: moving trucks</span>
You are moving boxes with rented trucks. Every truck on the road costs money, even if it
is half empty. You want **few, full trucks**. In our world, trucks are servers, boxes are
tasks, and sending a truck home means letting a server sleep.
</div>

Classic packing rules:

| Rule | How it chooses | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| **First Fit** | the first server, in a fixed order, that has room | fills the "front" servers; the back ones stay asleep |
| **Best Fit** | the fullest server that still has room | keeps loads high; empty servers drain |

Our world differs from moving trucks in one big way: **tasks leave on their own when they
finish**, and new ones keep arriving. So servers slowly empty out over time.
**Consolidation** means helping that along: move the last few tasks off a nearly empty
server so it can go to sleep sooner.

## A8. LR-MMT, step by step

LR-MMT is a well-known energy-saving method from research (Beloglazov and Buyya, 2012).
In our simulator it is Rohan's code (`ROHAN_Task/policies.py`). It has two parts.

**Part 1: the controller.** Every 5 minutes it looks at every server and does three things.

1. **Overload check with LR (Local Regression).** It draws a trend line through the
   server's last 10 CPU readings (50 minutes) and predicts the next one. The server
   counts as **overloaded** if the prediction × 1.2 reaches 100%, or if memory reaches 90%.
   (The 1.2 is a safety margin.)
2. **MMT (Minimum Migration Time).** From an overloaded server, move the tasks that are
   quickest to move (the smallest memory) until it is no longer overloaded.
3. **Underload.** A server using less than **30%** of both CPU and memory is
   **emptied**: if all of its tasks fit on other running servers, they are moved, and
   the empty server falls asleep.

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Tiny example: the trend line</span>
A server's last 10 CPU readings climbed steadily from {{ v.lr_ok_first }} to {{ v.lr_ok_last }}.
The trend line predicts {{ v.lr_ok_pred }} next. Times 1.2 gives {{ v.lr_ok_press }}, which is under 100%: **not
overloaded**. If the readings had climbed to {{ v.lr_over_last }} instead, the prediction would be
{{ v.lr_over_pred }}, and {{ v.lr_over_pred }} × 1.2 = {{ v.lr_over_press }}: **overloaded**, so the smallest tasks move away.
</div>

**Part 2: placement.** Between controller checks, every new task goes to the server where
it adds **the fewest extra watts**. This is called power-aware best fit. As a formula, the
**score** of a server is:

<div class="formula">score = (watts after placing − watts before) ÷ (full watts − idle watts)</div>

The lowest score wins. Dividing by ({{ v.full_w }} − {{ v.idle_w }}) = {{ v.scale }} W just turns watts into a number
between 0 and about 1. A sleeping server counts as {{ v.sleep_w }} W "before", so waking one looks
expensive.

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Tiny example: scores for one typical task (HPE)</span>

{{ t.score_table }}

The busy server wins. Waking a sleeping server is about {{ v.wake_vs_busy }} times more expensive, so
**LR-MMT only wakes a server when no running server has room**.
</div>

## A9. The memory-aware version

The proposal's idea was to make placement care about **memory too**, not only CPU, so that
neither resource is wasted (the "Tetris" idea: fit shapes together). Rohan implemented it
by adding a **balance term** to the score:

<div class="formula">memory-aware score = power score + 0.25 × | CPU share − memory share |  (after placing)</div>

Here | x | means "the size of x, ignoring the sign". A server whose CPU and memory are
equally full gets no penalty. A lopsided server gets a penalty.

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Tiny example</span>
A server at 30% CPU and 57% memory: | 0.30 − 0.57 | = 0.27, so the penalty is
0.25 × 0.27 ≈ **0.068**. Remember this number; it is about to cause trouble.
</div>

## A10. How "better" is measured

| Measure | What it tells you | Better is |
|---|---|---|
| **Energy** (kWh) | the electricity bill; the main goal | lower |
| **Mean wait** | average seconds from arrival to start | lower |
| **P95 wait** | 95% of tasks waited less than this | lower |
| **Pressure** | share of server-time above 90% CPU or memory; the team's warning sign for crowding | lower |
| **Migrations** | how many times tasks were moved (each move pauses a task) | lower |
| **Wake-ups** | how many times a server was woken | lower |
| **Servers on** | average number of servers switched on | lower |

In this simulator a crowded server does not actually slow down: requests can never exceed
capacity. Pressure is therefore a **warning sign**, not real slowness, but the team uses it,
so we report it.

<h1><span class="part">Part B</span>What D5 found (pen and paper only)</h1>

## B1. The starting results

The team's comparison (`BASELINE_Comparison`, 9 cases = 3 sizes × 3 server models) found:

- The memory-aware version saves a little energy on small workloads but uses
  **{{ v.team_mem_range }} more energy** on the largest workload.
- **First Fit**, the simplest rule, used the least energy in **{{ v.team_ff_wins }} of 9** cases.
  Plain LR-MMT used the least in none.

My D5 analysis showed *why*: the memory-aware version keeps more servers switched on at
the same time, because it spreads tasks out. On the largest workload, about
**{{ v.d5_running_share }}** of its extra energy is servers kept on while running work (mean of the 3
server models). Below, the cause is worked out on paper.

## B2. Why the memory-aware version spreads tasks out

Take one typical task (half a core, {{ v.task_mem_pct }} of memory) and two HPE servers:

- **Busy**: 30% CPU, 57% memory. It will stay on for a long time anyway.
- **Empty**: on, but with no work. Left alone, it **falls asleep within {{ v.idle_timeout }} seconds**.

{{ t.flaw1_table }}

The memory-aware rule picks the **empty** server, and that is a costly choice:

1. The busy server's imbalance (0.27) **was already there**. This one task changes it by
   only {{ v.task_skew }}. The penalty is mostly a **tax on the server's existing state**,
   not on the decision being made.
2. That tax ({{ v.flaw_balance_gap }}) is **{{ v.flaw_ratio }} times bigger** than the whole power difference between
   the two servers ({{ v.flaw_power_gap }}).
3. The real cost of choosing the empty server is large: it **keeps a server on that would
   have slept**, which costs {{ v.on_premium }} W for as long as the task runs. The score sees
   only {{ v.empty_extra_w }} W.

It gets worse with every task of the same job. Each task placed on a server adds a little
more imbalance there, so the **next** task of the job looks cheaper on a *different* empty
server. The memory-aware version therefore spreads one job's tasks across several servers
(Part C3 shows this with the real code), and each of them stays on until its share of the
job ends.

<div class="box why" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Why the score gets this wrong</span>
A score that compares choices should measure **what each choice changes**. The power part
does ("watts after − watts before"). The balance part does not ("imbalance after").
Repairing that score is a possible next step (Part F3). This guide takes a different,
simpler route: it leaves every score alone and adds one rule about **which tasks share a
server**.
</div>

<h1><span class="part">Part C</span>The new idea: keep each job together</h1>

## C1. Why a server stays on

A server can only go to sleep when its **last** task finishes. So what keeps a server on
is often a single leftover task.

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Tiny example</span>

| Server | Tasks on it | When it can sleep |
|---|---|---|
| S1 | 40 tasks of job J7, all started at 2:00 | when J7 ends |
| S2 | 30 tasks of J7 and 1 task of job J9 | when J7 **and** J9 have both ended |

If J9 runs an hour longer than J7, S2 stays on for that extra hour just for one task. Had
that task been placed with the other J9 tasks, S2 could have slept as soon as J7 ended:
{{ v.sleep_w }} W instead of {{ v.idle_w }} W or more for that hour.
</div>

All the tasks of a job are created at the same moment, and they usually finish close
together (C4 checks this on the team's data). So a server that holds **one** job tends to
empty **in one go**.

## C2. The same-job rule

<div class="formula">Put a new task next to tasks of its own job, if a suitable server exists.</div>

"Suitable" means three things, and each one is there for a reason:

| Condition | Why it is needed |
|---|---|
| 1. The server is **running work** right now | The rule must never wake a sleeping server or refill an empty one. (The policy is not told when a task finishes, so its notes can be a few minutes out of date.) |
| 2. It already has **tasks of this job** | That is the whole idea: job-mates finish together. |
| 3. It stays **under the overload line** after this task (memory 90%, CPU forecast × 1.2) | Overcrowding would make the controller move the job's tasks apart again, the opposite of the goal. |

If several servers qualify, the algorithm's own score picks among them. If **none**
qualifies, the algorithm decides **exactly as before**. That is the whole rule. It is
added in front of Rohan's code for both LR-MMT and the memory-aware version
(`RUSHI_Task/same_job/policy.py`), and:

- the **controller** is Rohan's, untouched (LR overload check, MMT, the 30% underload rule);
- there are **no new numbers**: the overload line already exists in Rohan's controller;
- it needs **no prediction**: every task already carries its job's id;
- **switch the rule off and you get Rohan's policy exactly** (a unit test compares every
  result field).

## C3. A tiny example, worked out by the real code

Job J7 arrives with {{ v.toy.tasks }} identical typical tasks. Five HPE servers are on: two busy
ones (**Busy-A**: 30% CPU, 57% memory; **Busy-B**: 45% CPU, 50% memory) and three with no
work (**Empty-1** to **Empty-3**, which would fall asleep within {{ v.idle_timeout }} s if left alone). The
tasks are fed one by one to Rohan's real code, with and without the rule:

{{ t.toy_table }}

- The **memory-aware** version spreads J7 over **{{ v.toy.mem_servers }} servers**, all of them empty ones that
  would otherwise have slept (the arithmetic of Part B2, repeated task after task). With the
  rule, J7 sits on **{{ v.toy.mem_rule_servers }}**, and the other {{ v.toy.mem_saved }} empty servers fall asleep.
- **Plain LR-MMT** already prefers busy servers, so it keeps no extra server on. The rule
  only keeps J7 on one busy server instead of {{ v.toy.lr_servers }}.

That is why we expected a much bigger effect on the memory-aware version than on plain
LR-MMT, before running anything.

## C4. Does the idea's assumption hold?

The rule only makes sense if tasks of the same job really finish close together. We checked
on the team's own large sample, using {{ v.premise.pairs }} pairs of tasks from {{ v.premise.jobs }} jobs:

| Pairs of tasks | Median gap between finish times | Finish within 60 s of each other |
|---|---:|---:|
| Same job | **{{ v.premise.same_gap }}** | **{{ v.premise.same_within }}** |
| Different jobs, released in the same 5 minutes | {{ v.premise.other_gap }} | {{ v.premise.other_within }} |

**Yes, strongly.** Tasks of the same job finish almost together. 60 seconds is the
simulator's idle timeout, so tasks that end within it let their server sleep in one go.
(This check reads the recorded durations; no policy ever sees them.)

## C5. Is it new?

We searched the research literature (a web search, not a full systematic review):

| Published work | What it does | How ours differs |
|---|---|---|
| MinUsageTime bin packing (Li, Tang & Cai and follow-ups) | Groups work by finish time to empty servers, but assumes finish times are **known in advance** | We do not know finish times |
| Resource Central (Microsoft, 2017); lifetime-aware placement (Microsoft, 2023) | **Machine learning** predicts how long each virtual machine lives | No prediction model |
| LAVA and NILAS (Google, 2025) | Group machines by **predicted** lifetime; put short ones on servers kept on by long ones | No prediction; the job itself is the signal |

What we did **not** find anywhere: using **job membership itself** as the "will finish
together" signal for energy-saving placement. That is the part we can propose as new,
with the words "to our knowledge".

<h1><span class="part">Part D</span>Testing fairly on the team's data</h1>

## D1. What "tuning on the data" means

<div class="box example" markdown="1">
<span class="label">Analogy: the practice exam</span>
If you memorize last year's exam answers, you will ace last year's exam and learn very
little about this year's. Tuning a scheduler on the same data you test it on is the same
trap. You end up learning the quirks of that data, not a better rule. This is called
**overfitting**.
</div>

A sneakier version: try ten variants, keep the one that looks best, and report only that
one. Even if every variant is reasonable, picking the winner after seeing the results
makes it look better than it really is.

## D2. Our four rules

1. **Design on paper.** The rule comes from how the simulator works (a server sleeps only
   when its last task leaves) and from one check of the trace (C4), not from trying versions
   on the data and keeping the best.
2. **No new numbers.** The rule has nothing to adjust. Its only threshold, the overload
   line, already existed in Rohan's controller.
3. **Fix the plan before running.** The rule, the comparisons, the success rule and our
   predictions were written down and committed to git before the first run (commit
   `{{ v.first_design_commit }}` on my working branch). For the pull request the code was moved into its own
   folder and all {{ v.runs }} runs were repeated; every result matched the first run exactly.
4. **Run once, report everything.** Each policy ran once on each case, and every
   comparison is reported, including the ones that fail.

<div class="box why" markdown="1">
<span class="label">One honest caveat</span>
D5 used these same 9 cases, so this is not a test on data nobody has seen. What stops it
from being "tuning on the data" is rules 1 to 3: nothing was adjusted after seeing a result.
</div>

## D3. The data: only the team's

- The **same 9 cases** as the team's comparison (`BASELINE_Comparison`): 250, 1,000 and
  4,000 task groups, each run on the three server models separately.
- The **same tasks** ({{ v.team_tasks[250] }} / {{ v.team_tasks[1000] }} / {{ v.team_tasks[4000] }} for the three sizes),
  the same 64 servers and the same simulator settings. The prepared files are checked
  against the team's fingerprints before every run.
- Rohan's two original policies were **re-run** with a read-only counter attached (it counts
  how many different jobs share each server). All {{ v.originals_reproduced }} re-runs matched the team's committed
  results in every field, so the counter changed nothing. First Fit and Best Fit use the
  team's committed results.

## D4. What counts as success (decided before running)

The **performance limits** are the team's own, from their earlier experiment
(`POLICY_Improvement/config.json`), not chosen by us:

- mean wait and P95 wait may rise by at most **1 second or 5%**, whichever is larger;
- pressure may rise by at most **0.01** (one percentage point).

A policy **works better** than another if **all three** hold:

1. it uses less energy in **more than half** of the 9 cases (at least 5);
2. its **total** energy over the 9 cases is lower;
3. it stays **within the limits in every case it wins**.

| ID | Comparison | The question it answers |
|---|---|---|
| S1 | LR-MMT + rule vs LR-MMT | Does the rule improve LR-MMT? (main) |
| S2 | Memory-aware + rule vs memory-aware | Does the rule improve the memory-aware version? (main) |
| S3 | LR-MMT + rule vs First Fit | Does it now beat the simplest rule? |
| S4 | Memory-aware + rule vs First Fit | The same question for the memory-aware version |
| S5 | Memory-aware + rule vs LR-MMT | The team's question: does the proposal (with the rule) beat LR-MMT? |
| S6 | Memory-aware + rule vs LR-MMT + rule | With the rule on both, does caring about memory help? |

## D5. The 36 runs

| What | Runs |
|---|---:|
| LR-MMT + rule and memory-aware + rule × 9 cases | 18 |
| Rohan's two originals re-run with the counter × 9 cases (must match the team's results) | 18 |
| **Total** | **36** |
| First Fit and Best Fit × 9 cases: the team's committed results | (18 already done) |

Every run uses a **fresh simulator** and the team's own checks (`validate_run`): every
task finishes, nothing is rejected, durations are unchanged except for migration pauses,
and the energy parts add up to the total.

